WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.164
A useful GPU cost model looks beyond the visible price of a compute instance.

00:00:05.164 --> 00:00:10.227
The workload may consume GPU capacity, attached CPU and memory, local disks, remote volumes,

00:00:10.227 --> 00:00:13.482
object storage, snapshots, network transfer, managed orchestration, logging, monitoring,

00:00:13.482 --> 00:00:16.014
and support time from engineers and operators.

00:00:16.014 --> 00:00:19.269
Price and effective cost are not the same thing.

00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:23.248
A lower-priced resource that sits idle, waits on storage, fails repeatedly,

00:00:23.248 --> 00:00:28.673
or requires manual recovery may be more expensive than a better-matched resource that completes reliably.

00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:33.736
Effective cost asks how much useful output the team receives for the money spent.

00:00:33.736 --> 00:00:35.545
Capacity choices also have tradeoffs.

00:00:35.545 --> 00:00:38.076
On-demand usage can be simple and flexible.

00:00:38.076 --> 00:00:43.863
Reserved or committed capacity can help predictable demand but may create waste if the workload changes.

00:00:43.863 --> 00:00:48.927
Interruptible or preemptible capacity can reduce spend when jobs can tolerate stops and restarts,

00:00:48.927 --> 00:00:51.459
but it needs checkpointing and retry design.

00:00:51.459 --> 00:00:53.990
Hidden costs often live around the job.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.161
Large datasets may be copied repeatedly.

00:00:56.161 --> 00:00:57.246
Checkpoints may accumulate.

00:00:57.246 --> 00:01:00.139
Logs and metrics may grow without retention rules.

00:01:00.139 --> 00:01:04.479
Artifacts from old experiments may remain long after the decision they supported.

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:07.373
Operational labor should be part of the picture.

00:01:07.373 --> 00:01:10.628
If a workload requires frequent manual cleanup, investigation, reruns,

00:01:10.628 --> 00:01:13.521
or emergency budget review, that effort has cost.

00:01:13.521 --> 00:01:17.861
A good model explains both what the platform charges and how well

00:01:17.861 --> 00:01:20.031
the workload uses what it allocates.

00:01:20.031 --> 00:01:24.372
It also helps teams compare alternatives without pretending that the listed compute

00:01:24.372 --> 00:01:26.180
rate is the whole story.
